                         Gallinelli et al. /                                                                      3

1.3. Methodology

   The first step was to define an investigation methodology and the qualitative criteria based on the multiple senses
that affect the complex and multisensory perception of pedestrians while roaming through the city (e.g. the human
body is sensitive to radiant temperature from surrounding surfaces, to air turbulence, to noise, to odors, to visual and
acoustic perception, and so on…).

   Then, knowing that the deployment of a fixed monitoring network with as many data collection points as
necessary would be too restrictive, too expensive and difficult to achieve, we changed the paradigm by developing a
portable and affordable measuring device in the form of a backpack. In this way, the sensors are attached to a
pedestrian operator and move along a free or predefined path through the city. This provides great flexibility of use,
anywhere and at any given time. The data is collected at a rate of 1 Hz and is made available for display and analysis
by the means of a dedicated graphical interface or more traditional analysis tools.

   Hemispherical and ground images complete the sensor array. They are used for analysis, such as sky view factor
or vegetation index, but also allow for ‘reverse engineering’ of acquired data: in a way they give eyes to the sensors
and allow better understanding of cause and effect. Data collected from the sensors feed a human body heat balance
model to evaluate physiological equivalent temperature (PET), which is defined as equivalent temperature having
comparable heat sensation in controlled environmental conditions without radiation, wind, etc. PET is easily
understandable and is closest to the actual heat sensation.

         Fig. 1. (left) operator during a climate walk, carrying the microclimate-meter, (right) sample of data displayed in the ad-hoc viewer

2. Description

2.1. Investigation and measurement equipment

   The specifically designed ‘microclimate-meter’, further referred to as uCM, is the core part of CityFeel. The goal
is to deploy an array of environmental sensors within a mobile and light-weight system.

   Thus, beyond the conventional parameters (ambient temperature and humidity), the uCM also logs multi-
directional radiation temperatures, vertical temperature profiles at pedestrian level, thermal turbulence, wind speed,
direction and turbulence, noise level and records the soundtrack and hemispherical images (sky and ground camera).
A GPS receiver and an inertial reference platform make it possible to locate the measured data in time and in space.
In the latest version we added additional sensors to measure air quality such as CO2, NOx, Ozone and micro
particles.
