            Relative humidity                             0.1°C       0.5°C
            Radiant temperature (left, right … up, down)  1W/m2       1.5%
            Solar radiation                               0.01m/s     1%
            Wind speed                                    1dB         3%
            Sound pressure                                0.1m        <10m
            Position (GPS)

2.2. The uCM on-board equipment

   The sensor array is connected to a dedicated logging equipment consisting of a Campbell Scientific CR-6 data
logger, an ATmega328 based micro-controller board and a nano-PC for image handling and on-the-fly data quality
assessment and synchronization. Ongoing development includes a real-time graphical operator interface.

   The approximate data flux is 7MB/second. The autonomy is about three hours of continuous sensor streaming,
limited only to LiPo battery and memory stick capacity and capacity of the operator to carry the 5kg equipment.

2.3. Data processing and results

   During the development process, calibration test walks followed a fixed reference sequence, characterized by a
sufficiently contrasting "urban scape" (avenue, square, park, street). At present, the operator simply puts on the uCM
and starts monitoring according to specific interests.

                        Fig. 2. Overview of the process of data analysis: crude data visualization, sectorization, statistical analysis

   Once the campaign is finished, the operator transfers the collected data to a desktop PC where a script aggregates
all collected data, converts it to a suitable format for post-treatment and calculates derived indicators related to
outdoor human heat stress, such as mean radiant and operative temperatures, PET (Physiologically Equivalent
Temperature), UTCI (Universal Thermal Climate Index), HUMIDEX (Humidity index), WBGT (Wet Bulb Globe
Temperature), etc.
A Java™ program allows for first observation and interpretation of the collected data by the means of a dedicated
graphical user interface. In depth analysis is done by traditional environments, such as e.g. ArcGIS mapping and
Peter   Peter Gallinelli    Eetnaelr.g/yEPnreorcgeydPiaro0c0ed(2ia01172)20(0200–1070)0391–396                                 395
       Gallinelli et al. /                                                                                                     5

analytics software, Mathlab®, Statistica® or R, typically used to sectorise, analyze and visualize data. Machine
learning algorithms take care of related image analysis, such as sky view factor (SVF) or vegetation indexes.

2.4. Case-studies

   The interaction of city and climate is a very complex process and is determined by many influencing factors.
Added to this are weather conditions, time of day, use (differences between works and holidays) and much more.
Never less, assuming that variations over a short period of time are small, a close look into measurements data give
clues to comprehension of the urban metabolism.

   How efficient are climate strategies in the real-world and what are the best options can be identified by the
observation of comparative solutions at different periods and times of the day. For example evaporation from water
effectively acts as a cooling strategy, as can be seen on the illustration hereafter.

             Fig. 3. Example effect of water as urban climatic design feature decreasing temperature whilst increasing air water content.

   Climate walks are also useful to identify places where heat stress and pollution are least. This knowledge can be
used to inform the population and to identify local adaptation and mitigation strategies. Charts showing parameters
such as PET, UTCI, humidity, wind, etc… show a strong correlation between urban morphology and parameters
affecting human outdoor comfort and health.

Fig. 4. PET profile of a climate walk in Geneva center indicating cold- and hot spots on a summer morning and sectors (right)
396  Peter   Peter Gallinelli    et al. / Energy Procedia 122  0(20001–70)00391–396
6           Gallinelli et al. /  Energy Procedia 00 (2017)

   It has been shown that already a single climate walk can be meaningful. The following comparison of trends for a
series of thirteen sectors (A to M, see figure 4 right side) on different days is very good. This means that even
though the absolute temperature may not be known in advance, the data of a single walk can be extrapolated to
predict thermal behavior of the city and give objective indications to questions such as: what are the best places to
rest, which are the most comfortable and healthiest ways to travel from one place to another depending on the time
of day, and so on.

                               Fig. 5. Comparison of temperature and PET profiles for different days; sectors refer to figure 4

2.5. Discussion and outlook

   The uCM monitoring and logging backpack has contributed in research projects related to effects of vegetation
and water in the city and urban morphology. Numerous climatic walkthroughs have been completed over the last
year revealing interesting insight into climatic metabolism of cities.

   The uCM is readily available for research in the field of city climate, city- and soundscapes (well-being) and air
quality (health). On the basis of ‘crowdsharing’ the uCM is made available for free to public and academic partners
who are interested in investigating, contributing and sharing studies through an open-data structure containing
monitored situations relating to climatic urban design of public spaces and air quality. The collected data will be
shared amongst the scientific community by the means of an open data structure. This knowledge will be useful in
applications such as urban planning, choice of urban surfaces, design of the “green city” and uses such as traffic
control, pollution prediction, health assessment as well as general urban management.

   The uCM is now in version 2.0 and ongoing hard- and software development aim to make the uCM ever more
immersive, easier and intuitive to use.

